[{:db/id 17592186105855 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b936ee5c-7e32-56c6-afd0-2cda76d77856" "question-0f0ef420-2cbc-5713-b114-6ede5820bdae"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105691 :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n![](images/03/03d1cadde8d86003bf86cbde2609b841731c55966fbdaa2b26988c2401ccf049.svg)\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n![](images/6a/6a6fb806e99ba4cddc944ebed697bc4ddcee2569d63d8ecae8dee8d5f1fc05c8.svg)\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get\n\n![](images/e5/e5b7b74863ccacf150b132919751c4314784e0bf01763d0e1a1cf2df0de5ce62.svg)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||\\mathbf r'(t)|| = \\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2}\\sin t)^2 + (\\sqrt{2}\\cos t)^2} = \\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\cos{t}$ and $y(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\sin{t},$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105856 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-97a1a962-e2cb-547c-a9e5-0ef1c288cefc" "question-f9ddb293-a141-5410-8588-0be8dbbb83c3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105692 :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n![](images/a6/a62b446c9a1b82ffd922f5708e84d12314afa02e9f7bdfe5e61f7d96670f1cea.svg)\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n![](images/98/98bf5b8b1812e95d5d749fa12292329b1a2ead3f7d1e73c8b9bde767c4535d9a.svg)\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n![](images/c2/c22e707e65334af655da1fb796fc1eae7665d9d56dd196282b3810941b450c3b.svg)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{\\sqrt{144{cos}^{2}⁡t+81{sin}^{2}⁡t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-b936ee5c-7e32-56c6-afd0-2cda76d77856" :question/id #uuid "b936ee5c-7e32-56c6-afd0-2cda76d77856" :question/math-academy-id "q-126914" :question/problem "The closed, positively oriented curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4b8d3a0d-de2c-5e6c-a792-1bd70490a272"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4b8d3a0d-de2c-5e6c-a792-1bd70490a272" :answer-field/id #uuid "4b8d3a0d-de2c-5e6c-a792-1bd70490a272" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6113e78e-3c31-5b91-be16-7271edc5b671" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c3fbf14d-6a97-5982-90b0-30f215c18cf2" "answer-1bf4ba5d-ef9f-5153-ae1c-e4c0c5782ebd" "answer-20fcf7c1-0e53-5bc4-9774-94258e3e649e" "answer-f7e65222-2412-514f-8cd8-601d4bfe9e96" "answer-6113e78e-3c31-5b91-be16-7271edc5b671"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3fbf14d-6a97-5982-90b0-30f215c18cf2" :answer/id #uuid "c3fbf14d-6a97-5982-90b0-30f215c18cf2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1bf4ba5d-ef9f-5153-ae1c-e4c0c5782ebd" :answer/id #uuid "1bf4ba5d-ef9f-5153-ae1c-e4c0c5782ebd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20fcf7c1-0e53-5bc4-9774-94258e3e649e" :answer/id #uuid "20fcf7c1-0e53-5bc4-9774-94258e3e649e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7e65222-2412-514f-8cd8-601d4bfe9e96" :answer/id #uuid "f7e65222-2412-514f-8cd8-601d4bfe9e96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6113e78e-3c31-5b91-be16-7271edc5b671" :answer/id #uuid "6113e78e-3c31-5b91-be16-7271edc5b671" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0f0ef420-2cbc-5713-b114-6ede5820bdae" :question/id #uuid "0f0ef420-2cbc-5713-b114-6ede5820bdae" :question/math-academy-id "q-126924" :question/problem "The closed, positively oriented curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6d5ad3f1-9a81-57d1-9a87-dc11af7a74bc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6d5ad3f1-9a81-57d1-9a87-dc11af7a74bc" :answer-field/id #uuid "6d5ad3f1-9a81-57d1-9a87-dc11af7a74bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d905a5f3-7922-5213-884e-760e5bcf3649" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4273bc0b-c236-5aef-b7ab-73105db3d922" "answer-a164f912-bf55-5a5b-ae4c-d8d627540a32" "answer-77b52dfd-4977-5787-b514-fbe4937870e3" "answer-d905a5f3-7922-5213-884e-760e5bcf3649" "answer-f0b60cba-f8e0-5442-8c96-6db838819306"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4273bc0b-c236-5aef-b7ab-73105db3d922" :answer/id #uuid "4273bc0b-c236-5aef-b7ab-73105db3d922" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a164f912-bf55-5a5b-ae4c-d8d627540a32" :answer/id #uuid "a164f912-bf55-5a5b-ae4c-d8d627540a32" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77b52dfd-4977-5787-b514-fbe4937870e3" :answer/id #uuid "77b52dfd-4977-5787-b514-fbe4937870e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "100\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d905a5f3-7922-5213-884e-760e5bcf3649" :answer/id #uuid "d905a5f3-7922-5213-884e-760e5bcf3649" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f0b60cba-f8e0-5442-8c96-6db838819306" :answer/id #uuid "f0b60cba-f8e0-5442-8c96-6db838819306" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-97a1a962-e2cb-547c-a9e5-0ef1c288cefc" :question/id #uuid "97a1a962-e2cb-547c-a9e5-0ef1c288cefc" :question/math-academy-id "q-126933" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5e6414f4-2314-56d1-97b2-1f02e11d8c75"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5e6414f4-2314-56d1-97b2-1f02e11d8c75" :answer-field/id #uuid "5e6414f4-2314-56d1-97b2-1f02e11d8c75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-439e9a02-8366-5fd0-88a4-aac15fafa034" :answer-field/choices ["answer-16e038fe-7a43-52bb-9c39-90c0889e7847" "answer-7b2f1542-170e-55d7-a28b-44d4c1502880" "answer-439e9a02-8366-5fd0-88a4-aac15fafa034" "answer-5e1f89cf-4b72-5a63-9cc0-b059a94c4c27" "answer-0437d5f4-9256-56dd-87fb-59350017483d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16e038fe-7a43-52bb-9c39-90c0889e7847" :answer/id #uuid "16e038fe-7a43-52bb-9c39-90c0889e7847" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b2f1542-170e-55d7-a28b-44d4c1502880" :answer/id #uuid "7b2f1542-170e-55d7-a28b-44d4c1502880" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-439e9a02-8366-5fd0-88a4-aac15fafa034" :answer/id #uuid "439e9a02-8366-5fd0-88a4-aac15fafa034" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e1f89cf-4b72-5a63-9cc0-b059a94c4c27" :answer/id #uuid "5e1f89cf-4b72-5a63-9cc0-b059a94c4c27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0437d5f4-9256-56dd-87fb-59350017483d" :answer/id #uuid "0437d5f4-9256-56dd-87fb-59350017483d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f9ddb293-a141-5410-8588-0be8dbbb83c3" :question/id #uuid "f9ddb293-a141-5410-8588-0be8dbbb83c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-126928" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=14\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}14\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}14\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}14\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 14\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 14\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+14\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3706a6bf-e668-594b-b09d-444f45050912"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3706a6bf-e668-594b-b09d-444f45050912" :answer-field/id #uuid "3706a6bf-e668-594b-b09d-444f45050912" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f95a11de-5734-527e-9084-6f608d51df00" :answer-field/choices ["answer-09ae1d17-586d-5656-9819-82e44f1f99e8" "answer-6627b401-f702-5235-8c84-b1a54bb3416f" "answer-672c97f7-89dc-5157-8a28-9704883db291" "answer-3f50700d-4949-56a2-a27b-18e517742a91" "answer-f95a11de-5734-527e-9084-6f608d51df00"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09ae1d17-586d-5656-9819-82e44f1f99e8" :answer/id #uuid "09ae1d17-586d-5656-9819-82e44f1f99e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6627b401-f702-5235-8c84-b1a54bb3416f" :answer/id #uuid "6627b401-f702-5235-8c84-b1a54bb3416f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-672c97f7-89dc-5157-8a28-9704883db291" :answer/id #uuid "672c97f7-89dc-5157-8a28-9704883db291" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f50700d-4949-56a2-a27b-18e517742a91" :answer/id #uuid "3f50700d-4949-56a2-a27b-18e517742a91" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f95a11de-5734-527e-9084-6f608d51df00" :answer/id #uuid "f95a11de-5734-527e-9084-6f608d51df00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107127 :tutorial/content "When $C$ is a closed curve, there is always a well-defined notion of \"inside\" and \"outside\" $C.$ It is usually easy to determine which region is inside or outside an arbitrary closed curve using our natural intuition.\n\nOne important quantity that we often need when dealing with closed, oriented curves is the **outward-pointing unit normal vector,** usually denoted $\\mathbf n$.\n\nAs the name suggests:\n\n- $\\mathbf n$ is normal (i.e., perpendicular) to the curve $C$ at *every* point,\n\n- it is a unit vector (i.e., its magnitude equals $1$), and\n\n- it always points in the outward direction.\n\nSince $\\mathbf n$ must be normal to $C$ at *every* point, it must vary as we move around $C.$ Therefore, it is usually written either as a function of the parameter space (i.e., $\\mathbf n(t)$) or as a function of the coordinates of the points on $C$ (i.e., $\\mathbf n(x,y)$). \n\nAs a concrete example, let's consider the closed, positively oriented ellipse $C,$ parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = 3\\cos t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 2\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\n![](images/fe/fea4c20453e9c80493f5c5431859fd7918d75cbaa494ad0b3deeccacfae24019.png)\n\nFor convenience, we first express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n![](images/c7/c7d514dbfdb746d94487a7ebca6b239173b854010eeccb80b4afc06cd09a9c0e.svg)\n\nLet's calculate the vector $\\mathbf n$ for this curve. To do this, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA typical tangent vector for our curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/10/10ae867f682158bce2098234210206d4d7eb811be780505ca1984fb9e9516a27.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n![](images/27/27e11f86245d4331e33cec34742a7b6c395ff22fe95bcf465ec569f7168b4c0f.svg)\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get the vector $\\tilde{\\mathbf n}(t),$ given by\n\n![](images/00/006458079641036b283371fb36a94b39b11dcb09d796175a555539287218d8a7.svg)\n\nThe vector $\\tilde{\\mathbf n}(t)$ is shown below:\n\n![](images/7c/7ca9a18d78783a923b63fcc41a8bc41ccab5c4a12bb00f5c305e35e2e1bc40e5.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nThe vector $\\tilde{\\mathbf n}(t)$ in an outward-pointing normal vector, but it is not a unit vector. Therefore, we need to normalize it.\n\nNotice that  \n\n$$\n||\\mathbf r'(t)|| = \\sqrt{(3\\sin t)^2 + (2\\cos t)^2} = \\sqrt{9\\sin^2 t + 4\\cos^2 t}.\n$$\n\nFurthermore, the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore, we can calculate our outward-pointing unit vector $\\mathbf n$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}(t)}{||{r}^{′}(t)||} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\dfrac{2\\cos t\\,\\mathbf i + 3\\sin t\\,\\mathbf j}{\\sqrt{9\\sin^2 t + 4\\cos^2 t}}\n$$\n\ngives us an outward-pointing unit normal vector to every point on our ellipse." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107129 :tutorial/content "Let's consider a general, closed curve $C$ in the plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = x(t)\\,\\mathbf{i} + y(t)\\,\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nWe will assume that $C$ is oriented counterclockwise. Our goal is to derive a general formula for $\\mathbf n.$ \n\nAs always, we first express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n![](images/48/48517c1b1d9915c8f739f8a3979778eb7ea8ddebe01f276034f8df1c301ce802.svg)\n\nTo calculate $\\mathbf n,$ we follow our usual three steps.\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n![](images/49/492e23c319bc0681cf1c65d0ff91fccdde02686891b3ae1c1daf9f512f38a7e7.svg)\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get the vector \n![](images/e4/e44acf1d3bdb1caaf25b89faa191a58faa7979881d9a7093ec23ea9ab8405103.svg)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNotice that  \n\n$$\n||\\mathbf r'(t)|| = \\sqrt{(x'(t))^2 + (y'(t))^2}.\n$$\n\nFurthermore, the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore, we can calculate our outward-pointing unit vector $\\mathbf n$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) \\\\ -{x}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\,i-\\frac{{x}^{′}(t)}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)\\,i-{x}^{′}(t)\\,j}{\\sqrt{({x}^{′}(t))^{2}+({y}^{′}(t))^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186106019 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
